  "candidate_0063": {
    "reason": "The assertion claims higher capacity results in better predictions as a general rule. This is factually unsound because model capacity alone does not guarantee improved performance; without sufficient training data, high capacity leads to overfitting where test accuracy degrades. A concrete counterexample is a small dataset (e.g., 100 samples) trained with a massive neural network (high capacity), which will inevitably perform worse than a smaller, appropriately sized model due to overfitting, directly refuting the unconditional 'results in better predictions' claim.",
    "sound_as_stated": false,
    "counterexample": "Training a high-capacity model on a small dataset leads to overfitting and lower generalization performance compared to a lower-capacity model."
  }
}
